          %title
          %p
            This page is meant to provide a general example and template for new
            = succeed "-pages." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HomePage"} nLab
            %strong
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/source/Template+page"} its source code
            to see how the various parts are done. A minimal template is given first which can be copy-and-pasted into newly created pages. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HowTo"} HowTo
            for more details.
          %h2#minimal_template_code Minimal Template Code
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                
                # Contents (or put a title here)
                * this block creates the table of contents, leave as is
                {: toc}
                
                ## Idea
                
                (...)
                
                
                ## Definition 
                
                (...)
                
                
                ## Properties
                
                (...)
                
                
                ## Examples
                
                (...)
                
                
                ## Related concepts
                
                * [[page name 1]], [[page name 2]]
                * [[page name 3]]
                
                
                ## References
                
                (...)
                
                
                [[!redirects alternative title]]
                [[!redirects alternative titles]]
          %p
            A more detailed example follows. Check out the source code
            %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/source/Template+page"} here
            to see how it’s coded:
          %hr/
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#abstract"} Abstract
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#special_cases"} Special cases
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#Specific_examples"} Specific examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            It is an old observation that xyz. One notices that from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            this is just an abc. This leads to the definition of a uvw. It is useful for doing klm and provides the basis for the more general theory of äöü.
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p
            A uvw is effectively a uv together with a w. Its main property is encoded in Somebody’s Theorem which says that it consists of precisely three letters. The archetypical example of a uvw is
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mi ν
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu \nu \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/template+page#Specific_examples"} special examples paragraph
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p As Jacques Distler said,
          %blockquote
            %p
              See
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/wiki/instiki/show/Theorems"} more about definition/theorem/proof-environments
          %p
          #uvwDef.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (uvw)
              %br/
            %p
              A
              %strong uvw
              is a UVW in which all letters are lower case.
            %p
          %p This may be summed up in the slogan:
          .standout
            %p A uvw is just what it looks like.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
            %p
              Every uvw (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#uvwDef"}
            %p
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p By inspection.
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p Every uvw contains strictly more than one letter.
            %p
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p Use the above lemma and continue counting:
            #eq:firstequation.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    1 + 1 = 2
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Every uvw (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#uvwDef"}
            %p
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
            %p
              Along the lines of the above proposition, we use equation
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:firstequation"} (1)
              and then conclude with
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2 + 1 = 3
                    \\,.
            %p
              Notice that this is indeed independent of in which order we sum up the letters, in that the following
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagram commutes
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mo ×
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mo ×
                            %mi ℕ
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi Id
                                %mo ×
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mo ×
                            %mi ℕ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                                  %mo ×
                                  %mi Id
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                            %mo ×
                            %mi ℕ
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{N}\times \mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}
                    \&amp;
                    \\overset{Id \times + }{\longrightarrow}
                    \&amp;
                    \\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{+ \times Id}}
                    \\big\downarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow^{\mathrlap{+}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}
                    \&amp;
                    \\underset{+}{\longrightarrow}
                    \&amp;
                    \\mathbb{N}
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
          .num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p No uvw contains more than three letters.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#special_cases Special cases
          %ul
            %li
              %p First case
            %li
              %p Second case
            %li
              %p Third case
          %h3#Specific_examples Specific examples
          %p For ease of reference, we will number the examples.
          %p
          #FirstExample.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p The first example is obvious.
          %p
          #SecondExample.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The second example is a slight variation of Exp.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FirstExample"}
          %p
          #ThirdExample.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The third example is completely different from both Exp.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SecondExample"}
          %h2#References References
          %p The original definition appeared in section 3 of
          %ul
            %li
              FirstName LastName,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Title
              %strong vol
              num (year) pages-pages (
              %a{:href => "http://put.url/here"}> arXiv:xxxxxx
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://another.url"} doi:yyyyyyy
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://yet.another.url/"} pdf
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/meta"} meta
